::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2<sup>3</sup>. {
=A. 8#Correct. 2<sup>3</sup> = 2 &middot; 2 &middot; 2 = 8.
~B. 6#This multiplies 2 by 3. An exponent means repeated multiplication.
~C. 9#This squares 3 instead of using 2 as the base.
~D. 5#This adds the base and exponent. Exponents do not mean addition.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 5<sup>0</sup>. {
~A. 0#This is a common misconception. A zero exponent does not make the value zero.
~B. 5#This keeps the base unchanged. A zero exponent changes the value to 1 for nonzero bases.
=C. 1#Correct. Any nonzero base raised to the zero power equals 1.
~D. 10#This adds 5 and 0 or doubles the base. Use the zero exponent property.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 3<sup>2</sup> &middot; 3<sup>1</sup>. {
~A. 9#This stops after evaluating 3<sup>2</sup>. The expression also includes 3<sup>1</sup>.
=B. 27#Correct. 3<sup>2</sup> &middot; 3<sup>1</sup> = 3<sup>3</sup> = 27.
~C. 18#This multiplies 3<sup>2</sup> by 2 instead of by 3<sup>1</sup>.
~D. 81#This adds too much to the exponent. The exponent should be 2 + 1 = 3.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 4<sup>3</sup> &divide; 4<sup>1</sup>. {
~A. 4#This subtracts exponents correctly but stops at 4<sup>1</sup>. The result should be 4<sup>2</sup>.
~B. 12#This divides the exponent 3 by 1 or combines numbers incorrectly. Use quotient of powers.
~C. 64#This evaluates only 4<sup>3</sup> and ignores the division.
=D. 16#Correct. 4<sup>3</sup> &divide; 4<sup>1</sup> = 4<sup>2</sup> = 16.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 16<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>. {
=A. 4#Correct. 16<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup> = &radic;16 = 4.
~B. 8#This divides 16 by 2. An exponent of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> means square root.
~C. 32#This multiplies by 2 instead of finding the square root.
~D. 256#This squares 16 instead of finding its square root.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 27<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub></sup>. {
~A. 9#This divides 27 by 3. An exponent of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> means cube root.
=B. 3#Correct. 27<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub></sup> = &#8731;27 = 3.
~C. 81#This multiplies by 3 instead of taking the cube root.
~D. 729#This cubes 9 or misuses the exponent. The rational exponent asks for a root.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 25<sup><sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>. {
~A. 15#This multiplies 25 by <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>. A rational exponent represents a root and a power.
~B. 75#This multiplies 25 by 3. Use the square root first.
~C. 625#This squares 25. The exponent is not 2.
=D. 125#Correct. 25<sup><sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup> = (&radic;25)<sup>3</sup> = 5<sup>3</sup> = 125.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 10<sup>-2</sup>. {
~A. -100#A negative exponent does not make the value negative. It creates a reciprocal.
~B. -20#This multiplies 10 by -2. Exponents do not mean multiplication by the exponent.
~C. 100#This ignores the negative exponent. The reciprocal is needed.
=D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub>#Correct. 10<sup>-2</sup> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>10<sup>2</sup></sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub>.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2<sup>3</sup> &middot; 2<sup>2</sup>. {
=A. 32#Correct. 2<sup>3</sup> &middot; 2<sup>2</sup> = 2<sup>5</sup> = 32.
~B. 12#This multiplies visible exponents or factors incorrectly. Use product of powers.
~C. 64#This multiplies exponents to get 2<sup>6</sup>. Add the exponents instead.
~D. 10#This adds 8 and 2 or combines values incorrectly.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (3<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 12#This multiplies the base and exponents incorrectly. Evaluate the power of a power.
~B. 18#This doubles 9 but does not square it.
~C. 729#This uses 3<sup>6</sup>. The exponent should be 2 &middot; 2 = 4.
=D. 81#Correct. (3<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 3<sup>4</sup> = 81.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 64<sup><sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub></sup>. {
~A. 8#This finds &#8731;64 but does not square the result.
=B. 16#Correct. 64<sup><sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub></sup> = (&#8731;64)<sup>2</sup> = 4<sup>2</sup> = 16.
~C. 32#This divides or multiplies by parts of the exponent. Use cube root then square.
~D. 128#This multiplies 64 by 2. Rational exponents represent roots and powers.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 7<sup>0</sup>. {
~A. 0#This is the common zero exponent misconception. The value is not 0.
~B. 7#This keeps the base unchanged. A zero exponent gives 1 for nonzero bases.
=C. 1#Correct. Any nonzero base raised to the zero power equals 1.
~D. 70#This treats the exponent as a digit. Exponents describe powers, not place value.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>2</sup> &middot; x<sup>6</sup>. {
=A. x<sup>8</sup>#Correct. The bases match, so add the exponents: 2 + 6 = 8.
~B. x<sup>12</sup>#This multiplies the exponents. Product of powers uses addition.
~C. 2x<sup>8</sup>#This adds an extra coefficient that is not in the original expression.
~D. x<sup>4</sup>#This subtracts the exponents instead of adding them.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite m<sup>9</sup> &divide; m<sup>4</sup>. {
~A. m<sup>13</sup>#This adds exponents. Quotient of powers uses subtraction.
~B. m<sup>36</sup>#This multiplies exponents. Use subtraction for division with matching bases.
=C. m<sup>5</sup>#Correct. m<sup>9</sup> &divide; m<sup>4</sup> = m<sup>9-4</sup> = m<sup>5</sup>.
~D. m<sup>4</sup>#This subtracts in the wrong order or drops too many factors.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite (p<sup>4</sup>)<sup>3</sup>. {
~A. p<sup>7</sup>#This adds the exponents. Power of a power uses multiplication.
~B. p<sup>1</sup>#This subtracts the exponents. Subtraction is for quotients with matching bases.
~C. p<sup>64</sup>#This raises 4 to the third power. Multiply the exponents instead.
=D. p<sup>12</sup>#Correct. (p<sup>4</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = p<sup>4 &middot; 3</sup> = p<sup>12</sup>.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite y<sup>-2</sup> with a positive exponent. {
~A. -y<sup>2</sup>#A negative exponent does not make the term negative. It creates a reciprocal.
=B. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>y<sup>2</sup></sub>#Correct. y<sup>-2</sup> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>y<sup>2</sup></sub>.
~C. y<sup>2</sup>#This changes the exponent sign without moving the factor to the denominator.
~D. <sup>y</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>#This treats the exponent like division by 2. Use y<sup>2</sup> in the denominator.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite a<sup>0</sup>, assuming a is not 0. {
=A. 1#Correct. a<sup>0</sup> = 1 for a not equal to 0.
~B. 0#This is the common zero exponent mistake. A nonzero base to the zero power equals 1.
~C. a#This keeps the base unchanged. The zero exponent property gives 1.
~D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>a</sub>#This is related to a negative exponent, not a zero exponent.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite (2x)<sup>3</sup>. {
~A. 2x<sup>3</sup>#This applies the exponent only to x. The grouping symbols include both 2 and x.
=B. 8x<sup>3</sup>#Correct. (2x)<sup>3</sup> = 2<sup>3</sup>x<sup>3</sup> = 8x<sup>3</sup>.
~C. 6x<sup>3</sup>#This multiplies 2 by 3 instead of cubing 2.
~D. 8x#This cubes 2 but does not cube x.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite (3x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 6x<sup>4</sup>#This multiplies 3 by 2 instead of squaring 3.
~B. 9x<sup>2</sup>#This squares 3 but does not apply the outside exponent to x<sup>2</sup>.
=C. 9x<sup>4</sup>#Correct. (3x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 3<sup>2</sup>x<sup>4</sup> = 9x<sup>4</sup>.
~D. 3x<sup>4</sup>#This applies the exponent to x<sup>2</sup> but not to the coefficient 3.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>5</sup> &middot; x<sup>-2</sup>. {
~A. x<sup>7</sup>#This adds 5 and 2 but ignores the negative sign.
~B. x<sup>-10</sup>#This multiplies the exponents. Product of powers uses addition.
=C. x<sup>3</sup>#Correct. Add the exponents: 5 + (-2) = 3.
~D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>x<sup>7</sup></sub>#This treats the whole result as negative. The exponent sum is positive.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>4</sup> &divide; x<sup>7</sup> with a positive exponent. {
=A. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>x<sup>3</sup></sub>#Correct. x<sup>4</sup> &divide; x<sup>7</sup> = x<sup>-3</sup> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>x<sup>3</sup></sub>.
~B. x<sup>3</sup>#This subtracts in the wrong order. The exponent is 4 - 7 = -3.
~C. x<sup>11</sup>#This adds exponents. Quotient of powers uses subtraction.
~D. x<sup>-3</sup>#This is an intermediate form, but the question asks for a positive exponent.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student says x<sup>3</sup> &middot; x<sup>4</sup> = x<sup>12</sup>. Which correction is best? {
~A. x<sup>3</sup> &middot; x<sup>4</sup> = x<sup>1</sup> because 4 - 3 = 1.#This uses subtraction, which applies to quotients, not products.
~B. x<sup>3</sup> &middot; x<sup>4</sup> = 2x<sup>7</sup> because there are two x terms.#This adds an extra coefficient. The bases combine into one power.
=C. x<sup>3</sup> &middot; x<sup>4</sup> = x<sup>7</sup> because product of powers uses addition of exponents.#Correct. Multiply powers with the same base by adding exponents.
~D. x<sup>3</sup> &middot; x<sup>4</sup> = x<sup>12</sup> because exponents multiply.#This repeats the misconception. Exponents are added for product of powers.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student says (a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = a<sup>5</sup>. Which correction is best? {
~A. (a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = a<sup>1</sup> because 3 - 2 = 1.#Subtraction is not used for a power raised to a power.
=B. (a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = a<sup>6</sup> because power of a power uses multiplication of exponents.#Correct. Multiply 2 and 3 to get 6.
~C. (a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = a<sup>9</sup> because 3<sup>2</sup> = 9.#This squares the outside exponent instead of multiplying 2 and 3.
~D. (a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = 3a<sup>2</sup> because the outside exponent becomes a coefficient.#An exponent outside parentheses does not become a coefficient.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to r<sup>-4</sup>? {
~A. r<sup>4</sup>#This changes the sign without moving the factor. A negative exponent creates a reciprocal.
~B. -r<sup>4</sup>#A negative exponent does not make the expression negative.
~C. <sup>r</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>#This treats the exponent like division by 4. The whole power belongs in the denominator.
=D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>r<sup>4</sup></sub>#Correct. r<sup>-4</sup> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>r<sup>4</sup></sub>.




}

::ALG1_U01_L06_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to q<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>? {
=A. &radic;q#Correct. An exponent of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> represents the square root.
~B. <sup>q</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>#This treats the exponent like division by 2. A rational exponent represents a root.
~C. q<sup>2</sup>#This squares q instead of taking the square root.
~D. 2q#This multiplies by 2. The exponent <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> does not mean multiply by 2.




}
